WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.950
(gentle music)

00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:03.300
- [Narrator] It's known
as Earth's evil twin.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:04.630
- Venus gives us a glimpse

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:07.200
into both our past and our future,

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.780
a past where water formed
and perhaps life arose

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:12.340
before it did on Earth,

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:15.070
and our future where Earth will heat up.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.370
So, Venus is a fascinating place

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:19.570
to look at Earth in a mirror.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:20.940
- [Narrator] The two planets are close

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.290
in age, size and makeup.

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:24.380
But unlike Earth,

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:27.190
Venus evolved into a
kind of toxic wasteland.

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:28.870
It lost its surface water

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:31.230
and became hot enough to melt lead.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.040
So, what happened?

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.300
Researchers are hoping
to answer that question

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.030
and whether Venus may have
once been brimming with life

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:40.353
with three new missions to the hot planet.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.010
- They will offer the
entire science community

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:48.040
the chance to investigate a planet

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.830
we haven't been to in more than 30 years.

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:52.060
- [Narrator] In those 30 years,

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:54.720
much of the attention has gone to Mars.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.350
- Mars is not like Earth and
Venus. Mars is much smaller.

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:01.900
It's only about four
seventh the size of Earth.

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.590
Venus turns out to be the other
planet in our solar system

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:06.250
which is the most Earth-like,

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:09.760
but yet a horrible fate
has befallen Venus.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.190
- [Narrator] Dr. Darby Dyar
is among a group of scientists

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:14.240
working on the new missions to Venus.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.060
She's part of the
scientific leadership team

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:17.600
for one called VERITAS.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.230
And it's set to launch
at the end of the decade.

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:21.850
The spacecraft will take a census

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:23.580
of the rock types on Venus.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.650
That data is essential for understanding

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:27.080
what happened to Venus,

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:30.280
and if it hosted life,
where it might've been.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:33.360
- Imagine that sometime
in the last billion years,

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.450
Earth was completely covered
by basalt. (chuckles)

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:39.070
The only thing that would stick
up from the basaltic ocean

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:42.010
if that happened, would be the
continents, the high places.

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:44.860
So, it's sort of like the
Noah's ark on a planet.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:47.270
- [Narrator] Basalt are
dark, fine-grained rocks,

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:50.150
the kind that line the
ocean floors here on Earth.

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:52.750
Continents are made up mostly
of a different kind of rock

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:54.820
called granite, which is lighter.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:56.590
Scientists are interested in granites

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:58.640
because they form in
the presence of water,

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.410
a key molecule for life.

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:02.940
On Venus, those are the two primary rocks

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:05.690
that VERITAS wants to locate and ID.

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:08.563
And it's gonna try to do
that as it orbits Venus.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:13.130
- It's staggering to me
that we know so little

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:15.460
about the surface of
Venus and what that means

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:17.390
is that anything we
find with these missions

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:19.650
is gonna be really amazing and exciting

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.210
and first of its kind.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:22.560
- [Narrator] That huge undertaking

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.110
comes down in part to this sensor.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.420
Before launch, Dyar and her collaborators

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.950
are training the device and
its machine learning brains

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:31.220
on Earth rocks.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.997
Let's take a look at
how that process works.

00:02:33.997 --> 00:02:35.280
(machine whirring)

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.260
First, Dyar collected
a diverse set of rocks.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:41.003
Then they were cut and ground.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.610
The samples are then sent to a lab

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:46.360
at the German Aerospace Center in Berlin.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:48.400
There, researchers heat up the rock chips

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.720
to more than 800 degrees Fahrenheit

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:53.143
in this so-called Venus Chamber.

00:02:53.143 --> 00:02:53.976
(switch clicks)

00:02:53.976 --> 00:02:56.710
It's meant to mimic the surface
temperatures on the planet.

00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:58.710
At such high temperatures,

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:01.170
rocks heat up so much that they glow.

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:02.550
The kind of light they give off

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:04.440
depends on what the rocks are made of.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:07.770
Dark basalts emit more
light than lighter granites.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:10.900
That light is captured by
the prototype VERITAS Sensor

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:14.140
through a special kind of glass
window on the Venus Chamber.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:16.410
Eventually when VERITAS reaches Venus,

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:19.490
its sensors will scan the
planet and compare the new data

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:22.010
to rocks they've studied here on Earth.

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:24.910
- The geological processes act the same

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:27.640
and therefore, the rock types,
we are fairly confident,

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:28.900
are going to be the same.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:30.010
- [Narrator] If VERITAS can map out

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:32.740
granite formations on Venus,
that could help scientists

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:34.810
figure out where to send future landers

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:37.210
to narrow down the
search for signs of life.

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:40.010
Habitability, that's the end game.

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:42.690
- Not only do we care about
habitability on Earth and Venus,

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:46.240
but we also care about the
habitability of objects

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:48.230
in other solar systems
throughout the universe,

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:50.060
which are called exoplanets.

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:52.560
And if there are other
Earth and Venus-like objects

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.430
out there, then maybe we're not alone.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:56.650
And that's a really important question

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:58.943
that we'd really love to understand.

00:03:58.943 --> 00:04:01.526
(bright music)

